for about five months i had to think of  a way to get bodies into the restaurant  our goal with filipino food is how can i  turn anything that i was embarrassed  about into something of a source of  pride with kamaya night it came about  because i remember being embarrassed  about eating with my hands or my dad  eating with his hands when my american  friends would come i would even beg him  please order a pizza or a hamburger so  it makes sense to hold it with your  hands and i asked how can we turn this  around we wanted to make it grand we put  banana leaves on the table we burned the  leaves it became aromatic we tried  different landscapes of how to plate the  food because you eat with your eyes  first and then it just grew from there  in order to really understand the food  you need to understand the people so you  need to get involved you need to  understand the process because every  process is different for example me  going to culinary school you never burn  garlic but when you're cooking filipino  food you toast that garlic because  that's going to impart a completely  different flavor i wouldn't have known  that from reading any of the cookbooks  unless i actually cook with lola's  i would have never known that and these  are things when you're trying to cook  somebody else's food it's like really  understanding and cooking with them and  really jumping in there we're going to  use that jew for the pansit the  drippings from the longanisa the onions  the garlic the lemongrass  you'll see the remnants of the chinese  in our food panset for us it also  symbolizes long life so you'll have it  at birthdays you'll have it for marienda  which is our mid-afternoon snack have it  for lunch it's really easy to have any  time one of the obstacles for filipino  restaurants was when filipinos would  come to restaurants if it's not like  what they grew up on it's somehow not  credible but i think part of us being  out here is educating on the different  kinds of an a